{"id": 39, "ts": 1783116464.4218051, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I appreciate the additional observations. We now know that Candidate\u202fA has no direct evidence of stress resilience, Candidate\u202fB shows a relatively weak memory for numbers (raising a concern for attention to numerical information), and Candidate\u202fD lacks any direct reliability data. These gaps weaken A, B, and D on two of our most critical criteria\u2014stress resilience and reliability/attention\u2014while Candidate\u202fC continues to have documented 100\u202f% reliability, calmness in crisis, rapid correct decision\u2011making, and a positive crew atmosphere. Although C\u2019s communication difficulty and limited eagerness for further education remain concerns, the new information reinforces that no other candidate currently matches C\u2019s safety\u2011critical profile. Therefore, I maintain my provisional recommendation for Candidate\u202fC.", "thoughts": "Now we have the needed info. Now we need to produce public message as Agent 2. We need to incorporate these new observations into our argument. Current leading candidate is C. We have new info: A's stress resilience unknown (no direct), B has weak memory for numbers (attention issue), D's reliability unknown (no direct). So these gaps mean C remains strongest. Also we have concerns about C's communication and adaptability. But overall C still best.\n\nWe should state our current recommendation (still Candidate C) and explain why new info supports that. Also maybe note that B's weak memory for numbers is a concern for attention, D's reliability unknown is a risk, A's stress resilience unknown is a gap. So C remains best.\n\nThus public message: I agree with C, note new info, keep vote C.\n\nNow produce final output with PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON."}
